        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "When to Dethatch Lawn",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/when-to-dethatch-lawn/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is dethatching the same as power raking?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Power raking is one way to dethatch. A power rake uses rigid steel blades or flails that slice into the thatch and lift it out, which suits thick thatch. Spring-tine dethatchers and tine attachments comb through the lawn more gently and suit light thatch. Vertical mowers or verticutters are similar to power rakes but cut deeper and are mostly used by professionals."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do grass clippings cause thatch?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Grass clippings are mostly water and soft leaf tissue that decompose within days to weeks. They return nutrients to the soil and do not add to thatch. Thatch is made of tougher material such as stems, runners, rhizomes and roots. Bagging clippings to prevent thatch removes free fertilizer without solving the actual problem."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I dethatch and overseed on the same day?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, and for cool-season lawns that is the usual approach. Dethatch, rake up the debris, optionally core aerate, then spread seed while the soil surface is exposed. Apply a starter fertilizer where your soil test and local rules allow, and keep the surface moist until the seed germinates. Early fall is the best time for the whole sequence."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I dethatch before or after aerating?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Dethatch first, clear the debris, then aerate. Dethatching opens the surface and removes material that would clog an aerator's tines and bury the cores. Aerating second loosens the soil underneath and gives seed and fertilizer more places to settle. Overseeding and fertilizing come last, once both steps are done and the debris is gone."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I dethatch with a regular rake?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A stiff leaf rake removes surface debris but not real thatch. A dedicated thatch rake, with sharp curved blades, can work through a light layer on a small lawn, though it is hard physical work. For anything larger than a small yard or thatch over half an inch, a power rake or tine dethatcher is far more practical."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does a lawn take to recover from dethatching?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most lawns look rough for one to two weeks. In the right season, Bermuda typically recovers in two to three weeks, while cool-season lawns dethatched in early fall usually look fuller within four to six weeks, especially if overseeded and fertilized. Dethatching outside the ideal window can stretch recovery much longer and invite weeds."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does a mulching mower cause thatch?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Mulching mowers cut clippings into small pieces that settle into the lawn and decompose quickly. They return nutrients without adding to thatch. Thatch comes from tough stems, runners and roots building faster than microbes break them down. Fertilizing too heavily, watering too often and acidic soil matter far more than how you handle clippings."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
