    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Dethatch only when the thatch layer is thicker than about half an inch, and do it when the grass is growing strongly enough to recover: late summer to early fall for cool-season grasses like Kentucky bluegrass, and late spring to early summer, after full green-up, for warm-season grasses like Bermuda and zoysia. Under half an inch, thatch is harmless or even useful, and yearly core aeration is usually all the control a lawn needs. Measure first with a trowel, because dethatching a lawn that did not need it does more damage than good.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: Over Half an Inch, During Peak Growth
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dethatching is rough on grass. Whether you use a spring-tine rake, a tine attachment or a power rake with steel blades, the machine tears through the lawn surface and pulls out dead material along with some live grass. That is useful when there is a thick mat smothering the soil and unnecessary when there is not. So the timing question really has two parts: does the lawn need it, and is the grass in a growth phase that lets it heal in a few weeks.
          </p>

          <p>
            The threshold most extension services use is half an inch. Below that, a thatch layer cushions the lawn, insulates the crowns from temperature swings and reduces soil compaction from foot traffic. Above it, the layer starts holding water and fertilizer near the surface, roots grow up into the thatch instead of down into the soil, and the lawn becomes more vulnerable to drought, insects and disease. Above an inch, problems are close to guaranteed.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Grass type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best window</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Avoid</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cool-season (bluegrass, fescue, ryegrass)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Late August to late September</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Summer heat, late fall, early spring green-up</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Warm-season (Bermuda, zoysia, St. Augustine)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Late spring to early summer, after full green-up</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Spring green-up, late summer and fall</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If your lawn passes the half-inch test and it is the right season, dethatch. If it fails the test, skip it and spend the effort on aeration, fertilizing and mowing correctly. Our <a href="/best-dethatcher/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best dethatcher picks</a> cover tools for both light raking and heavy thatch, but the first tool you need is a trowel or a sturdy kitchen knife.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-is-thatch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Is Thatch, and Is Some of It Good?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Thatch is the tight layer of living and dead stems, runners, rhizomes and roots that builds up between the green grass blades and the soil surface. It forms when the lawn produces this tough plant material faster than soil microbes can break it down. The material is high in lignin, the same compound that makes wood tough, which is why it decomposes slowly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Grass clippings are not the cause, despite the persistent myth. Clippings are mostly water and soft leaf tissue and break down within days to a few weeks. Leaving them on the lawn returns nutrients to the soil and does not build thatch. The real thatch builders are vigorous spreading grasses, heavy nitrogen fertilization that pushes excess growth, and conditions that slow decomposition.
          </p>

          <p>
            A thin thatch layer is actually useful. Up to about half an inch helps the lawn tolerate foot traffic, reduces water evaporation from the soil surface, and protects the growing points of the grass from heat and cold. Lawns with no thatch at all can feel hard underfoot and dry out faster. The goal is not zero thatch but a thin, healthy layer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Problems start as the layer thickens. Water and fertilizer get caught in the thatch rather than reaching the soil, so the lawn needs more of both. Roots grow up into the thatch, where they dry out quickly in summer and freeze more easily in winter. Insects such as chinch bugs and several turf diseases find a home in the layer. Mowers scalp more, because the lawn surface becomes spongy and uneven.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-check" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Do You Measure Thatch?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use a trowel, garden knife or flat spade to cut a wedge or plug about three inches deep from the lawn. Lift it out and look at the side. You will see green grass at the top, a brown, fibrous, spongy layer below it, and then soil. The brown layer is thatch. Measure it with a ruler, from where the green stops to where the soil starts. Compress it gently between your fingers; spongy, felt-like material that springs back is thatch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check at least five or six spots around the yard, including sunny and shady areas, high-traffic and quiet areas, and any patches that look thin or dry quickly. Thatch is rarely uniform. A lawn may have a full inch in a sunny bluegrass area and almost none in a shady fescue corner, and you may only need to dethatch part of it. Push the plugs back into place afterward and water them; they will reroot.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Thresholds</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Under 1/2 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Normal, leave it</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1/2 to 3/4 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Aerate yearly or light dethatch</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Over 3/4 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Dethatch in season</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Over 1 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Remove over two seasons or renovate</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The lawn gives other clues that justify checking. A spongy feel when you walk on it, water running off the surface rather than soaking in, dry patches that do not respond to watering, and a mower that sinks and scalps in places all point to thatch. None of these alone confirms it, since compaction and poor soil cause some of the same symptoms, but they tell you to get the trowel out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Distinguish thatch from mat or from a buildup of loose debris on top. Loose dead grass and leaves on the surface can be raked off any time and are not thatch. Thatch sits tight against the soil and is woven with roots and runners. Also note any layering below the thatch, such as a distinct band where sod was laid on different soil. That is a soil problem, and core aeration helps it more than dethatching.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-grass-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. When Should You Dethatch Each Grass Type?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Some grasses build thatch quickly and some almost never do. The spreading grasses are the main offenders because their runners and rhizomes are tough, slow-decomposing material. Kentucky bluegrass, creeping bentgrass, Bermuda, zoysia and St. Augustine all produce thatch readily, especially when fertilized heavily. Bunch grasses such as tall fescue and perennial ryegrass rarely build significant thatch, and those lawns seldom need dethatching.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Grass</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Thatch tendency</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">When to dethatch if needed</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Kentucky bluegrass</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Late August to September</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Creeping bentgrass</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Late August to September</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tall fescue</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rarely needed; early fall</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Perennial ryegrass</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rarely needed; early fall</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bermuda</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Late spring to early summer</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Zoysia</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Late spring to early summer</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">St. Augustine</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate to high</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Late spring; use light methods only</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Centipede</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Late spring, if needed</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For cool-season lawns, late summer to early fall is best, roughly late August through September in most of the north and into early October in the transition zone. The goal is to finish at least four weeks before your first expected frost so the grass can recover. Fall also means fewer weed seeds germinating in the opened turf, and it is the right time to overseed thin spots, which you often need to do after dethatching.
          </p>

          <p>
            Early spring is a fallback for cool-season lawns but a weaker one. The grass is just waking up, crabgrass is about to germinate into any bare soil you expose, and summer heat follows before new growth fully establishes. If you must dethatch in spring, use a light method, do it as soon as the lawn is actively growing, and plan your crabgrass control carefully.
          </p>

          <p>
            For warm-season lawns, dethatch once the grass is fully green and growing vigorously, typically late spring to early summer. Bermuda recovers quickly and tolerates aggressive dethatching. Zoysia recovers more slowly, so give it the full early-summer window. St. Augustine has thick above-ground runners that power rakes can rip out, so treat it gently, raise the blades, and favor aeration or a light raking where possible.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="aerate-or-dethatch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Should You Aerate or Dethatch?
          </h2>

          <p>
            They solve different problems and are often confused. Dethatching removes a layer of organic material sitting on top of the soil. Core aeration removes plugs of soil to relieve compaction underneath. Aeration also helps thatch gradually, because the soil in the plugs mixes into the thatch and brings in microbes that decompose it, but it does not remove much thatch directly.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What you find</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What to do</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Thatch under 1/2 inch, soil loose</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Neither; keep mowing and feeding sensibly</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Thatch under 1/2 inch, soil compacted</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Core aerate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Thatch 1/2 to 3/4 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Core aerate yearly; light dethatch if it keeps building</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Thatch over 3/4 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dethatch, then core aerate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Thatch over 1 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dethatch in stages over two seasons, or renovate</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For lawns with moderate thatch, annual core aeration is often the better choice. It is less damaging, it also fixes compaction, and over a couple of seasons it can bring a half-inch-plus layer back under control. Our guide on <a href="/when-to-aerate-lawn/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">when to aerate your lawn</a> covers the timing windows and soil moisture, and they line up closely with the dethatching windows here.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you do both, the usual sequence is dethatch first, clean up the debris, then core aerate, then overseed and fertilize. Dethatching opens the surface, aeration opens the soil, and seed falls into both. It is a lot of disturbance at once, so do it only in the ideal window for your grass, and only when the measurements justify it. A <a href="/best-lawn-aerator/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">good core aerator</a> handles the second step.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thatch over an inch is a special case. Removing it all in one pass usually tears out most of the living grass with it. Many turf specialists recommend removing it gradually over two seasons, or, if the lawn is in poor shape anyway, treating it as a renovation: strip, prepare the soil and reseed or resod.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-dethatch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Do You Dethatch Without Wrecking the Lawn?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mow the lawn shorter than usual first, but not to the point of scalping. For most cool-season lawns that means cutting to around two inches. The soil should be slightly moist, not wet and not bone dry. Wet turf tears out in clumps, while dry soil is hard on the machine and the grass. Water lightly a day or two before if it has been dry, and mark sprinkler heads, since dethatcher blades and tines can damage them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose the tool to match the thatch. A spring-tine dethatcher, either a manual rake or a tine attachment for a mower, is enough for light thatch and touch-up work. A power rake or vertical mower with rigid steel blades is the right tool for thick thatch. Set blades so they just reach the soil surface, cutting about an eighth of an inch into it at most; deeper settings damage roots without removing more thatch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Make one pass across the lawn and check the results before making a second pass at an angle. You will pull up a surprising amount of material, and the lawn will look rough. That is normal. Rake or collect the debris; a <a href="/best-lawn-sweeper/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">lawn sweeper</a> speeds up the cleanup on larger lawns. Do not leave piles on the grass; they smother it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Recovery is where the timing pays off. Overseed thin areas immediately, water to keep the surface moist, and fertilize lightly to push regrowth. For cool-season lawns, an early-fall dethatch followed by the fall feeding schedule in our guide on <a href="/when-to-fertilize-lawn/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">when to fertilize your lawn</a> usually brings the lawn back thicker within four to six weeks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect the lawn to look worse before it looks better. A properly dethatched lawn often looks thin and brown for one to two weeks. Bermuda is typically back in two to three weeks in summer, while bluegrass in fall might take four to six. If you dethatched outside the right window, recovery is slower and weeds have more chance to take hold.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prevention" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Often Should You Dethatch, and How Do You Keep Thatch Away?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dethatch only as often as measurements say, which for most lawns is every few years at most and for many lawns never. A healthy lawn with active soil life breaks down thatch roughly as fast as it forms. Measure each year when you are planning fall or spring lawn work. If the layer is creeping back above half an inch within a year or two, something in your lawn care is pushing growth faster than decomposition, and that is the thing to fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Excess nitrogen is the most common culprit. Heavy, frequent fertilization with quick-release products produces lots of stems and runners. Reduce rates, use slow-release products, and keep yearly nitrogen within the range recommended for your grass. Frequent light watering also encourages shallow roots and a moist surface layer that builds thatch, so water deeply and less often, roughly an inch per week in one or two sessions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Soil conditions matter too. Acidic soil, with a pH well below about 6, slows the microbes that decompose thatch; a soil test tells you whether lime is needed. Compacted soil and heavy use of certain insecticides that reduce earthworm activity also slow decomposition. Earthworms are one of the best natural thatch control systems a lawn has.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mowing at the right height and frequency, removing no more than a third of the blade at a time, helps everything else work. Leaving clippings is fine and often helpful. Core aeration once a year on thatch-prone grasses, and occasional topdressing with a thin layer of compost or soil, keeps the layer in check. Do those consistently, and the dethatcher will spend most of its life in the shed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Dethatching without measuring first</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many lawns raked hard every spring have less than a quarter inch of thatch. Dethatching them tears out healthy grass and opens the soil to weeds for no benefit. Cut a plug, measure the spongy brown layer, and only dethatch areas where it is clearly over half an inch thick.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Dethatching in early spring</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cool-season lawns are just waking up in early spring, and the bare spots you create line up with crabgrass germination. Warm-season lawns are still partly dormant. Wait for the grass's peak growing window, early fall for cool-season grass and late spring to early summer for warm-season grass.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Setting the blades too deep</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Power rake blades only need to reach the soil surface. Setting them an inch into the ground rips out roots and runners and leaves the lawn looking plowed. Start shallow, make one pass, check how much thatch came up, then lower the blades slightly only if you need to.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Removing a thick layer all at once</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thatch over an inch is woven with living runners and roots. Pulling it all out in one session usually takes most of the grass with it. Remove it in stages over two seasons, combine it with core aeration, or, if the lawn is already thin, plan a full renovation.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping recovery care</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A freshly dethatched lawn is thin and stressed. Leaving it without seed, water or fertilizer invites weeds to fill the gaps. Overseed thin areas right away, keep the surface moist until seed germinates, and apply a light feeding to push regrowth during the weeks that follow.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is dethatching the same as power raking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Power raking is one way to dethatch. A power rake uses rigid steel blades or flails that slice into the thatch and lift it out, which suits thick thatch. Spring-tine dethatchers and tine attachments comb through the lawn more gently and suit light thatch. Vertical mowers or verticutters are similar to power rakes but cut deeper and are mostly used by professionals.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do grass clippings cause thatch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Grass clippings are mostly water and soft leaf tissue that decompose within days to weeks. They return nutrients to the soil and do not add to thatch. Thatch is made of tougher material such as stems, runners, rhizomes and roots. Bagging clippings to prevent thatch removes free fertilizer without solving the actual problem.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I dethatch and overseed on the same day?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and for cool-season lawns that is the usual approach. Dethatch, rake up the debris, optionally core aerate, then spread seed while the soil surface is exposed. Apply a starter fertilizer where your soil test and local rules allow, and keep the surface moist until the seed germinates. Early fall is the best time for the whole sequence.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I dethatch before or after aerating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Dethatch first, clear the debris, then aerate. Dethatching opens the surface and removes material that would clog an aerator's tines and bury the cores. Aerating second loosens the soil underneath and gives seed and fertilizer more places to settle. Overseeding and fertilizing come last, once both steps are done and the debris is gone.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I dethatch with a regular rake?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A stiff leaf rake removes surface debris but not real thatch. A dedicated thatch rake, with sharp curved blades, can work through a light layer on a small lawn, though it is hard physical work. For anything larger than a small yard or thatch over half an inch, a power rake or tine dethatcher is far more practical.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a lawn take to recover from dethatching?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most lawns look rough for one to two weeks. In the right season, Bermuda typically recovers in two to three weeks, while cool-season lawns dethatched in early fall usually look fuller within four to six weeks, especially if overseeded and fertilized. Dethatching outside the ideal window can stretch recovery much longer and invite weeds.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a mulching mower cause thatch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Mulching mowers cut clippings into small pieces that settle into the lawn and decompose quickly. They return nutrients without adding to thatch. Thatch comes from tough stems, runners and roots building faster than microbes break them down. Fertilizing too heavily, watering too often and acidic soil matter far more than how you handle clippings.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

