        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Dethatch when a measured thatch layer is thicker than about half an inch, and do it during your grass's peak growth: early fall for cool-season lawns, late spring to early summer for warm-season lawns. Under that threshold, leave the thatch alone or manage it with yearly core aeration.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The real fix for thatch is the lawn care that prevents it: moderate nitrogen, deep and infrequent watering, healthy soil pH and regular mowing. Measure every year, dethatch only when the trowel says so, and follow it with seed, water and a light feeding. Do that, and you may only need a dethatcher once a decade.
          </p>
        </div>

